# tools/ales_stereo_sweep_witness.rish — stereo_sweep: the filter sweep (a moving low-pass) carried into stereo, the same schedule on both channels — the sixth rung of the stereo EQ / filter class (ALES175).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES175.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-081412_fill-ales175-lotus-stereo-sweep.md
# Stands on: lotus/sweep.rye (ALES44 — the mono filter sweep run once per channel, its SweepError reused whole and its check_sweep reused for the up-front atomic validation), lotus/tone.rye (ALES40/ALES43 — the fixed and
# carried-state low-pass the sweep interpolates between, the degenerate-law equality target), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip).
#
# lotus/stereo_sweep.rye moves a low-pass's cutoff across a stereo master: it runs ALES44's proven mono sweep_low_pass on BOTH channels with the SAME schedule — num_from to num_to over den, in blocks blocks — so the moving
# brightness opens on both speakers together. Each channel opens its OWN i64 filter state and threads it across its OWN blocks, so the two channels never share a filter memory; the shared schedule preserves the stereo image for
# free, since a sweep's endpoints and block count are values the caller names rather than scalars measured across the field. A degenerate schedule (num_from == num_to) equals ALES40's fixed low_pass on both channels byte for byte
# (the carried state makes the block partition invisible per channel); a settled constant sweeps to itself on both. It validates the two endpoints, the span, and the block count once against the shared length before either channel
# is mutated (BadCoeff on an illegal endpoint, BadRange on a span past the samples, BadBlocks on an illegal partition), so a refusal never sweeps one channel and leaves the other torn; SweepError reused whole. Values only, never a
# length; the two channels leave balanced. The crux is four laws: the stereo sweep law (each channel equal to ALES44's mono sweep_low_pass with the same schedule byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing), the degenerate law
# (a degenerate sweep equal to ALES40's fixed low_pass on both, a settled constant sweeping to itself), the image law (an identical-channel master staying identical through the sweep), and the balance / silence / atomicity /
# degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_sweep_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-sweep: Language EN — stereo_sweep moves a low-pass's cutoff across a stereo master by running ALES44's proven mono sweep_low_pass on both channels with the same schedule: num_from to num_to over den, partitioned into blocks blocks. Each channel opens its own i64 filter state and threads it across its own blocks, so the two channels never share a filter memory, and the shared schedule preserves the stereo image for free, since a sweep's endpoints and block count are values the caller names rather than scalars measured across the field. A degenerate schedule, num_from equal to num_to, equals ALES40's fixed low_pass on both channels byte for byte, because the carried state makes the block partition invisible per channel; a settled constant sweeps to itself on both, a constant being a fixed point of every legal coefficient. It validates the two endpoints, the span, and the block count once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never sweeps one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced."
say "ales-stereo-sweep: Style Radiant — the stereo EQ and filter class holds its tone control (ALES170), shelf (ALES171), band-pass (ALES172, isolate the middle), notch (ALES173, drop the middle), and bell (ALES174, turn the middle); every one of those holds one brightness for a whole span. The gesture a musician reaches for as often as a fixed cut is the sweep — a cutoff that moves across the span, the filter opening or closing over a phrase: the slow open on a pad, the close into a drop. ALES44 built that for a mono clip, the first automated parameter in the suite, a value that changes over time under a schedule, seamless because ALES43's filter state carries across every block so a boundary never re-transients; a degenerate sweep equals ALES40's fixed low_pass over the whole span byte for byte. This rung carries it into stereo the way the class asks — run ALES44's proven sweep on each channel with the same schedule, so the moving brightness opens on both speakers together. Like every parametric edit, a sweep's schedule is fractions and a count the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same schedule on each channel preserves the stereo image for free, with no linking and no measurement; each channel sweeps its own content, which is exactly right for a filter — an identical-channel, mono-in-stereo master stays identical because the same input through the same schedule is the same deterministic output, while a panned master shapes each side by its own spectrum. Unlike the stateless shelf, notch, and bell, the sweep does carry filter state, yet each channel opens its own state and threads it across its own blocks, so the two channels never share a filter memory, as a real stereo filter never leaks one side's history into the other. The crux is four laws: the stereo sweep law (each channel equal to ALES44's mono sweep_low_pass with the same schedule byte for byte, the two channels genuinely differing so no channel is crossed); the degenerate law (a degenerate sweep equal to ALES40's fixed low_pass on both channels byte for byte, a settled constant sweeping to itself on both); the image law (an identical-channel master staying identical through the sweep, the shared schedule holding the image); and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law (an all-silent master sweeping to silence on both, the lengths held, an illegal endpoint, an out-of-range span, and an illegal block count each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count zero the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-sweep: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right), each swept through ALES44's own bounded block partition (max_sweep_blocks equal to 4096), the filter state at full i64 precision per channel; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a sweep writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the two endpoints, the span, and the block count are validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (ALES44's check_sweep, exported as a one-word additive pub for this up-front atomic check), so a refusal never sweeps one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoSweepError is ALES44's SweepError (BadCoeff, BadRange, BadBlocks) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault. The stereo sweep law is proven by running both stereo_sweep and ALES44's mono sweep_low_pass on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the degenerate law proves a degenerate schedule equals ALES40's fixed low_pass on both channels byte for byte and a settled constant sweeps to itself on each; the image law proves an identical-channel master stays identical through the sweep; and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the three refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the coefficient over sample indices rather than crossovers in hertz, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_sweep"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_sweep.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-sweep: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_sweep.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-sweep: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-sweep: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-sweep' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-sweep: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-sweep line"
